course: religion-ethics
lesson: 85
title: Pretend, cover stories and lies that become habits
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Pretend play is not lying when everyone is in on the game, but cover stories, show-off fibs and "I'm fine" when you're not can quietly grow into a habit.
explain: Charlie told his class he'd been to Disneyland; he hadn't, and now kids keep asking about the rides, so every answer needs a new lie, like a web that gets bigger and stickier.
explain: Scientists at University College London found in 2016 that when people told small self-serving lies again and again, the brain's alarm centre reacted less each time, and the lies grew bigger.
explain: Jesus said, "Whoever can be trusted with very little can also be trusted with much" (Luke 16:10): small honesty is training for big honesty, and small lies are training too.
explain: The way out is to own up early, use honest words for the same goal ("she can't come to the phone right now"), and tell someone you trust when "I'm fine" isn't true.
fact: Pretend play like the tooth fairy is shared imagination; for a little one it is the parents' call, and if he asks you seriously, send him to his parents.
fact: Psychologists find lies often need more lies to protect them; owning up early ("I made it up, sorry") ends the web.
fact: "Like clouds and wind without rain is one who boasts of gifts never given" (Proverbs 25:14).
fact: Saying "I'm about to" just to get someone off your back misleads them; James warns against confident talk about plans (James 4:13-15).
fact: If a parent asks you to lie, the vicar says: "Honour your parents (Exodus 20:12), yet our first obedience is to God, who asks truthfulness of us (Acts 5:29)."
fact: "She can't come to the phone right now" is true and polite: the same goal reached honestly.
fact: "Those who flatter their neighbours are spreading nets for their feet" (Proverbs 29:5); empty praise can make people overconfident.
fact: "I'm fine" is OK on an ordinary day, but when things are hard, tell someone you trust: "Carry each other's burdens" (Galatians 6:2).
fact: An embarrassing truth is often funny rather than hurtful, like "the dog got the pasty before me!"
fact: "Truthful lips endure for ever, but a lying tongue lasts only a moment" (Proverbs 12:19); if the truth is harmless, tell it.
fact: A 2016 study at University College London found the part of the brain that sounds an alarm reacted less to each repeated lie; Aristotle said we become what we repeatedly do.
fact: Most faiths prize truth highly and allow at most narrow exceptions: Islam and Judaism both allow softening the truth only to make peace between people who have fallen out.
fact: A 2010 study found most people told few lies or none on a given day, and a small group told half of all the lies; "Do not follow the crowd in doing wrong" (Exodus 23:2).
fact: Slang check: "fixin' to" (Texas) means about to; "slaps" means is great; "my lover" in Cornwall and the West Country is a friendly word for dear.
exercise: Noah, 4, asks Amira, 9, "Is the tooth fairy real?" What is the wise answer? | It's a family game; if he asks seriously, send him to his parents | Tell him straight away that it's all a lie | Tell him the tooth fairy will stop coming if he asks | Laugh at him for believing in fairies
exercise: Charlie said he'd been to Disneyland, and kids keep asking about rides. What is best? | Own up early: "I made it up, sorry" | Make up more stories about the rides | Stay home from school until they forget | Say his cousin went, so it nearly counts
exercise: Why does one show-off lie grow? | Each lie needs more lies to protect it | Because lies are always about holidays | Because classmates never ask questions | Because the first lie was too small
exercise: Bubba in Texas says he's "fixin' to" clean his room. What does "fixin' to" mean? | About to | Repairing | Refusing to | Finished
exercise: Is "I'm about to" a lie if you don't mean it? | Yes; saying it just to get someone off your back misleads them | No; words about the future can never be lies | Only if you say it more than three times | Only if you are talking to a teacher
exercise: Mum whispers, "Tell Aunt Sue I'm out," but she's in the kitchen. What honest words reach the same goal? | She can't come to the phone right now | She's gone to the moon, call back later | She's in the kitchen and doesn't want to talk | She died last week, sorry
exercise: What does the vicar say when a parent asks you to lie? | Honour your parents, yet first obey God, who asks truthfulness | Always do whatever a parent asks, even a lie | Never speak to your parents about it | Tell the whole street what your mum said
exercise: Jordan says, "Just say my song slaps, bro." What does "slaps" mean? | Is great | Is loud | Is short | Hits people
exercise: Why is a lie to boost Jordan's confidence unhelpful? | Empty praise can make him overconfident and embarrassed later | Because songs made by kids are never really good enough to deserve any praise at all | Because he'll stop playing music if praised | Because bands only listen to their fans
exercise: Lena says "I'm fine" every day, but she's being left out at lunch. What should she do? | Tell someone she trusts, because things are hard | Keep on saying "I'm fine" every day, so that Mum doesn't worry | Stop going to lunch at all and hide in the library | Leave everybody else out at lunch too, so they see how it feels
exercise: Rosie's dog ate Gran's pasty. Gran asks, "Did you like it, my lover?" What is the best answer? | Biscuit got it before me! Can you make another? | Yes, it was delicious, thank you, Gran, the best one ever | No, I didn't like it at all | I threw it away because it was cold
exercise: What does "my lover" mean when Gran in Cornwall says it? | Dear, a friendly word | Boyfriend or girlfriend | A pasty with extra gravy | The name of Gran's dog
exercise: Grace's brother wants her to say he was at the library, but he was at the skate park, which is allowed. What is best? | Just tell Mum the truth, she won't mind | Say he was at the library, it's harmless | Say he was at a friend's house instead | Tell Mum he ran away from home
exercise: What did the 2016 University College London study find about repeated lies? | The brain's alarm centre reacted less each time and lies grew | Each lie made people feel much worse than the last one, until they stopped lying for good | People who lied stopped lying after three days | Lying made people better at maths
exercise: Hamza says his imam taught you may soften the truth to make peace. What do most faiths say? | They prize truth and allow only narrow exceptions, like making peace | They allow any lie as long as it helps you | They forbid any kindness in speech at all | They say truth matters only on holy days
exercise: Ivy hears "everyone lies loads every day". What did a 2010 study find? | Most people told few or none on a given day; a small group told half | Every person tells at least a hundred lies a day, even small children and grandparents | Nobody has ever told a lie in a study | Children lie more than all grown-ups put together
exercise: What does Exodus 23:2 say? | Do not follow the crowd in doing wrong | Follow the crowd wherever it goes, to stay safe | Always do what most people around you are doing | Never go anywhere at all with a crowd of people
test: Your mate says, "One fib about Disneyland can't grow, it's tiny." What would most likely happen? | Kids ask about the rides, so he needs more fibs to cover it | Nothing; nobody will ever ask about it | Everyone will want to hear about it, so they will all buy him a real trip to Disneyland next summer | The teacher will give him a gold star
test: "Ma, I'm fixin' to do it!" An hour later Bubba's still on his tablet. What is the honest version? | After this game, Ma, and then I'll really do it | I've already done it, Ma, so you can go and check it | I'm never doing it, Ma, because it's your room anyway | I'm fixin' to do it right now, honest, Ma, I promise
test: "Oi, just tell her I'm out," Mum hisses, waving the potato peeler. What is the gentle, honest move? | Say "She can't come to the phone right now", and later tell Mum the lie felt wrong | Say "She's out" and forget about it | Hand Mum the phone and run away | Tell Aunt Sue that Mum is hiding from her
test: Theo says, "Little lies are nothing, they don't train you for anything." What did scientists find? | Repeated small lies made the brain react less and lies get bigger | Little lies make your brain stronger and smarter, like a muscle that gets trained every day | Little lies have no effect on the brain at all | Big lies come first, and small lies come later
test: A podcast says, "Everyone lies loads, so it's fine." What two mistakes are in that? | Most people lie little, and common doesn't mean good | Everyone does lie loads, and that makes it fine for everyone | Podcasts are always right about everything | Nobody lies at all, so the podcast is lying
test: Lena's mum asks, "You alright, love?" Lena's had a rotten week. What would happen if she always says "fine"? | She'd be left alone with the problem, and nobody could help | The problem would go away by itself | Mum would know anyway, without being told, and would fix it all secretly at school | Her friends would stop leaving her out
test: Kai's brother: "If you don't say my song slaps, you're dead to me, bro." What's the honest, kind reply? | The chorus is catchy, the drums need work; I'll help you | It slaps, best song ever, bro, you'll be famous by next year and on the telly | It's rubbish, give up music now | Shut up, I'm not listening to your song
test: A cartoon shows a kid who blames the pasty-eating dog on the postman. What is the honest fix? | Tell Gran the dog got it; she'll probably laugh | Blame the postman, because he can't defend himself | Say the pasty never arrived in the post at all, Gran | Buy a new pasty and pretend it's the one Gran made
test: Hamza says, "My imam said you can soften the truth to make peace." Your mate says, "So lying's fine for anything." What's wrong? | The exception is narrow, for peace, not for convenience | Nothing; any lie at all is fine for anything if an imam says so | Muslims are never allowed to tell the truth | Only Christians think honesty matters
test: Noah, 4, keeps asking, "Is the tooth fairy REAL real?" and looks worried. What should Amira do? | Send him to his parents, because he is asking sincerely | Tell him she's watching him all the time | Tell him the tooth fairy steals teeth from children who ask too many questions | Say she doesn't know who the tooth fairy is
test: Why do cover stories for harmless things make no sense? | If the truth is harmless, telling it costs nothing, and the lie risks trust | Because cover stories always work perfectly | Because Mum always prefers a story | Because skate parks are always banned
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the findings of the 2016 University College London study concerning repeated self-serving falsehoods? | The alarm response of the brain diminished with each repetition, and the falsehoods progressively increased in magnitude | The alarm response of the brain intensified with each repetition, and the falsehoods ceased | Repeated falsehoods produced no measurable alteration in the brain | Repeated falsehoods were found to improve the participants' memory
exam: Given that a parent instructs a child to state falsely that the parent is absent, identify the response most consistent with this lesson. | A truthful and courteous formulation, such as "She cannot come to the telephone at present" | A falsehood exactly as instructed, the parent's authority being absolute in all matters within the household | A refusal to answer the telephone on any occasion whatsoever | A public complaint to the caller concerning the parent's conduct
exam: Select the statement consistent with the teaching recorded in Luke 16:10. | Whoever can be trusted with very little can also be trusted with much | Whoever is untrustworthy in little matters will be trustworthy in great ones | Small matters are of no consequence to trustworthiness | Trust ought to be extended only in respect of great matters
exam: Ascertain which statement concerning the frequency of lying is consistent with the 2010 study cited. | The majority of persons told few or no falsehoods on a given day, a small minority accounting for half of all falsehoods | Every person told numerous falsehoods each day without exception | No person told any falsehood throughout the period of the study | Children were found to be responsible for the entirety of falsehoods recorded
